A common live-coding prompt, worked through end to end — Java, imperative then refactored:

```java
// Before: manual loop, mutable accumulator, nested condition
List<String> activeUserEmails = new ArrayList<>();
for (User u : users) {
    if (u.isActive() && u.getEmail() != null) {
        activeUserEmails.add(u.getEmail().toLowerCase());
    }
}

// After: named pipeline, no mutable accumulator
List<String> activeUserEmails = users.stream()
    .filter(User::isActive)
    .map(User::getEmail)
    .filter(Objects::nonNull)
    .map(String::toLowerCase)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

Notice the refactor splits the original single compound condition (`isActive() && getEmail() != null`) into **two separate filter stages** rather than one combined predicate — each stage now reads as one clear check, and the `null` guard sits right next to the operation (`toLowerCase`) it's protecting, instead of being bundled into a distant `if`. That's the general refactoring move interviewers are checking for: not just calling `.stream()`, but decomposing the original condition into the smallest meaningful stages.
